Based on the excerpts provided, accidental breakage of fixed glass (including windows) forming part of the Insured Property is covered under the Additional Benefit "Accidental breakage of glass and sanitary fixtures," if Home is shown on your Certificate of Insurance [RAA Contents PDS, page 52]. The excerpts do not specify who caused the breakage (e.g., a family member or child), and no exclusion listed limits cover based on who accidentally caused the breakage. The specific exclusions listed are:

- Repairing scratches (where the break does not extend through the entire thickness of the item);
- Glass in a glasshouse or greenhouse;
- Repair or replacement of any tiles or tiled fixture or splashback (whether tiled or glass) forming part of the Home;
- Toilet seats;
- Glass forming part of a stove, heater, oven or cook top as a result of the direct application of heat;
- A picture tube or screen of a television or visual display unit; or
- Glass forming part of a radio or clock, vase, ornament, lamp or photo frame [RAA Contents PDS, page 52].

Since a glass door is not listed among these exclusions, and the breakage is described as accidental (rather than intentional), based on the excerpts provided, this would appear to fall within the scope of the Additional Benefit, provided the glass door is "fixed glass forming part of the Insured Property" and Home is shown on your Certificate of Insurance [RAA Contents PDS, page 52].

**Caveat:** This benefit is also subject to the General Exclusions on pages 53–60 of the PDS, which are not included in the excerpts provided. I cannot confirm whether any of those broader General Exclusions might affect this specific scenario (e.g., involving a family member or child), as that content is not available to me. I recommend checking those pages or confirming with RAA directly for a complete answer.
